    {
      "id": 159,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        964.8960279788644,
        569.0886476603035
      ],
      "size": [
        451.4932965975155,
        155.09384205294066
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 9,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "KJ Node Use",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "We use the \"Reverse Image Batch\" node to flip the batch around and then use the \"ImageFromBatch\" node to select the first image in the flipped batch, which is the last image of the input video.\n\nA lot of the documentation that I found said that we could use -1 in \"ImageFromBatch:\" to select the last image, however I had a lot of issues with that and found using the KJ Nodes specific \"Reverse Image From Batch to be much more stable."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
    {
      "id": 160,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        2729.5760362892283,
        1103.283644579555
      ],
      "size": [
        421.91018508597017,
        141.3950489097183
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 10,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "title": "KJ Node Use",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Here we use the KJ Node specific Image Batch Multi to join the original clip to the extended clip. It is important that when we input the original clip, we ensure that we do not grab the reversed version of it.\n\n*The reversed version will not cause an error, but the first portion of the video will play in reverse."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    }
